rican public  space which speech are you talking about  yes  uh state of the union just this last one  right so you just changed your party  affiliation about three weeks ago yes  because uh it seems like all the  democrats are doing now is dirty gutter  politics and they're talking about the  country being split i think they're the  ones that split in the country just to  get to be back on top since bush won  over gore  thanks for the call anything more for  her well politicians in both parties  tend to split the country i remember  back in the nixon administration we had  a term positive polarization  and the idea of positive polarization  was basically to accentuate the issues  that divided the opposition  and essentially that's  you know forcing people into a divided  and competitive mode and any serious  politician does this and i can  understand that a southern democrat  might say well i can't take the  democrats anymore a lot of northern  republicans in my neck of the woods  can't take the bush version anymore  a little item in today's new york times  intelligence panelists that the last two  have been named to the  the panel that's charged with looking  into the intelligence that was used to  justify the iraq war those will be  charles vest who has served as president  of mit since 1990 and henry rowland  professor emeritus of public policy at  stanford  i heard your commentary on npr this week  about the co-chairs of this which  includes chuck robb and lawrence  silberman why are you so angry  frustrated disapproving at the of the  choice  well i don't care much about chuck robb  and i don't think the white house does  either he's not going to rock anybody  the problem is with larry silberman now  in the course of all the research i did  for a couple of the chapters of american  dynasty i was quite familiar with the  the circumstances of both the iran  conference scandal and the october  surprise scandal so the minute i hear  that larry silberman who's a senior  appellate court judge an old-timer in  the republican party was selected to be  the republican co-chair of this i  immediately uh all kinds of lights start  flashing because larry silberman uh has  been mentioned very specifically in the  book firewall that larry walsh who was a  special prosecutor for iran contrary now  larry walsh was always very credible to  me because my father knew him and he was  a deputy attorney general republican in  the eisenhower administration and he  flat out criticized larry silverman as  having been on the periphery of the  october surprise meeting with  representatives of the iranian  government about the hostage's release  from the standpoint of not having them  released and then also specifically  walsh criticized silberman  for when he was on the appeals court in  in the washington dc circuit uh  basically going beyond any  semblance of judicial behavior in trying  to wipe out the the conviction of oliver  north and the iran contra affair and on  one of the pages of walsh's book he  talks about how he was almost going to  prepare a an argument that there was  judicial misconduct on silberman's part  and send it to the house judiciary  committee and i suppose that might have  been a prelude to trying to get him  impeached he didn't do it in the end but  anybody who wants to read about this get  a hold of larry walsh's book firewall  and look up lauren silverman that panel  also has john mccain who's gotten a lot  of press for his selection to that panel  what do you believe its ultimate outcome  will be with this makeup i don't think  that there's going to be much outcome at  all i think the principal outcome will  be that they get it all past the  election as far as the committee is  concerned but i don't think it will get  past the election as far as the public  and the opposition are concerned next  call for kevin phillips is from  minneapolis you're on the air good  morning i just had a question regarding  a  historical matter i wanted to find out  what your views were about the use of  the lincoln quote that you used on in  one of the books and how a number of  historians came out and said that it it  was untrue and and not a correct quote  um the second issue is  relating to the bush administration  and  how much of our domestic policy is  skewed by them one way or another i mean  we got a 2.4  trillion dollar flood federal budget  economists have picked out about 100  billion of going to corporate welfare  i'm not really seeing a very good link  between  uh let's say the wealthy in america one  percent getting 99 percent  of a benefit of a 2.4 trillion dollar  federal budget  when the the best economist can come up  with 100 billion last question was i was  wondering your position in the nixon  administration  well my position was very short-lived i  worked in the campaign and then i worked  as special assistant for the us attorney  general but after my book the emerging  republican majority came out i was  basically too hot to handle so i had  very little to do and they acquiesced  into me resigning and i went out to  become a commentator at that point on  the notion of the the unfairness of the  economic program i completely agree  everything i've looked into about the  bush's history is  the the four generations have always  been involved either in the financial  side of the oil business or investment  firms banking firms brokerage houses and  they have a total commitment to the top  one percent economically now the the  first part of the question i'm  trying to remember exactly what it was  uh  you know i didn't take a note i'm sorry  um let me move on to another topic for  you which is uh before you arrived i  told people several times that uh and  the next sunday this sunday you will be  number two on the new york times  hardcover nonfiction list and here it is  right here on the page uh price of  loyalty ron suskind is a number one spot  the new york times also reviewed your  book michael oreskis wrote the piece i  just wanted to have you to respond to  one thing he wrote which is a reader is  tempted to jake phillips and say aren't  we all products of our forebears  well i suppose so but we're not all  president  so i can dispose of that particular  quote easily enough when we've got a  family that's produced the last two  republican presidents only divided by a  democratic president i think we want to  spend some time looking into the  forebears but one of the fascinating  things about the bushes  is that before george senior was elected  president in 1988 there was only one  biography of him only one  inquiry into his family and that was  written by a former aide of his at the  united nations and it's just  extraordinary that you can preempt as  successfully as that i guess nobody else  figured it was worth writing a biography  of him there was one out there already  we got into the white house without any  basic screening and then of course he  just got absolutely clobbered in 1992 so  nobody bothered to go back again because  they all figured he's gone this time  around for uh president bush that you  were able to turn to a number of books  about him that have been written what do  you think are the best  well  one of the best is the biography that  came out in 1998 by herbert parman i  mean this was during a period where i  suppose you might have thought that  george w was in motion but it wasn't  devoted to george w was pretty good in  terms of cataloging things about george  senior he just didn't pick up on them uh  i like parmet he wrote a book about  nixon that i thought was one of the  better books about nixon but he's pretty  easy on  really on republican presidents there  was a book  that i think it was called marching in  place by dan goodgame and another time  writer that came out in 1992.  it was a very good book in the sense  that it really exposed the shallowness  of the the policy apparatus under george  senior but it came out at a time when he  was already sliding down the tubes so it  didn't get much attention because with  his velocity down the tube you didn't  need to add too much more  some of the recent books have been quite  good about george w but i haven't kept  up with every last one of those  next is a telephone call from texarkana  texas you're on kevin phillips good  morning i enjoy your program thank you  for  being a voice for the world  uh a real american leaders on this  program i'm learning democracy work i i  must read your book i have a question i  have three but i'll limit it can you  please explain a bit about the george  bush's uh now defunct bankrupt company  our booth osama bin laden's investment  in that company was he repaid um  when the company went bankrupt  were his funds frozen at any time and  was 9-1-1  involved as a type of revenge or  anything in this involvement and  if you could speak about skull and bones  i won't mention the arms market  arms merchants involvement in the world  let me try to get our bus though because  i don't think we can bite off two huge  chunks here but the arbusto thing you're  referring to this is george w bush's  first oil venture in texas in the late  1970s  now he raised money through a  a texas businessman named james bath who  according to some sources had been  recruited by his father as a cia asset  and beth  if he was recruited it was because he  was the north american representative of  the bin ladens and the bin mafu's family  who were saudi bankers now at this point  in time osama bin laden would have been  just a kid i mean there's no osama bin  laden problem here at all at that point  but it does show the closeness of the  bushes to the saudis and the saudi royal  family and the saudi money people  i don't believe that they ever really  got any serious money back from their  investment in in our busto because as a  lot of people pronounced it slightly  differently it was pretty effectively  and pretty 